Usage: env [OPTION]... [NAME=VALUE]... [COMMAND [ARG]...]

Set each NAME to VALUE in the environment and print the
resulting environment, or run COMMAND in that environment.

  -f, --file           read variables from a .env-style file
  -S, --split-string   process and split S into arguments
  -a, --argv0          pass STRING as argv[0] to command
  -C, --chdir          change working directory

Signal options (accepted, limited Windows support):
  --default-signal     reset handling of SIG to default
  --ignore-signal      set handling of SIG to do nothing
  --block-signal       block delivery of SIG to COMMAND
  --list-signal-handling  list non default signal handling

OPTIONS:
  -i, --ignore-environment    start with an empty environment
  -u, --unset ARG             remove variable from the environment
  -0, --null                  end each output line with NUL, not newline
  -f, --file ARG              read and set variables from a .env-style
                              configuration file
  -S, --split-string ARG      process and split S into separate arguments;
                              useful for shebang
  -a, --argv0 ARG             pass STRING as argv[0] to the command
  -C, --chdir ARG             change working directory
      --default-signal[=ARG]  reset handling of SIG to its default
      --ignore-signal[=ARG]   set handling of SIG to do nothing
      --block-signal[=ARG]    block delivery of SIG to COMMAND
      --list-signal-handling  list non default signal handling
  -v, --debug                 print extra information about the processing
      --help                  display this help and exit
  -V, --version               output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
